2026-11-07T06:33:13.4534587Z 3. Create the system users [34m(uids [30001..30008])[0m and groups [34m(gid 30000)[0m
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4535296Z    that the Nix daemon uses to run builds. To create system users
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4535934Z    in a different range, exit and run this tool again with
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4536396Z    NIX_FIRST_BUILD_UID set.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4536596Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4537048Z 4. Perform the basic installation of the Nix files daemon.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4537413Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4537682Z 5. Configure your shell to import special Nix Profile files, so you
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4538171Z    can use Nix.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4538346Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4538487Z 6. Start the Nix daemon.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4538711Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4539084Z [32m[4;32mWould you like to see a more detailed list of what I will do?
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4539680Z [0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4542719Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4542852Z I will:
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4543582Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4544776Z  - make sure your computer doesn't already have Nix files
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4545154Z    (if it does, I will tell you how to clean them up.)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4545532Z  - create local users (see the list above for the users I'll make)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4545878Z  - create a local group (nixbld)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4546134Z  - install Nix in /nix
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4546383Z  - create a configuration file in /etc/nix
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4546729Z  - set up the "default profile" by creating some Nix-related files in
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4547042Z    /root
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4556214Z  - back up /etc/bash.bashrc to /etc/bash.bashrc.backup-before-nix
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4556814Z  - update /etc/bash.bashrc to include some Nix configuration
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4567006Z  - load and start a service (at /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.service
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4567456Z    and /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.socket) for nix-daemon
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4567792Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4568976Z [32m[4;32mReady to continue?
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4569435Z [0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4586249Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4586785Z [34m---- let's talk about sudo -----------------------------------------------------
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4596127Z [0mThis script is going to call sudo a lot. Normally, it would show you
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4596801Z exactly what commands it is running and why. However, the script is
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4597167Z run in a headless fashion, like this:
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4597335Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4597490Z   $ curl -L https://nixos.org/nix/install | sh
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4597800Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4598016Z or maybe in a CI pipeline. Because of that, I'm going to skip the
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4598631Z verbose output in the interest of brevity.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4598848Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4598963Z If you would like to
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4599252Z see the output, try like this:
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4599459Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4599647Z   $ curl -L -o install-nix https://nixos.org/nix/install
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4600077Z   $ sh ./install-nix
2026-11-07T06:33:13.4600276Z 
